% =========================================
% ABSTRACT
% =========================================
\begin{abstract}
    Adaptive Bitrate (ABR) streaming algorithms face a fundamental ``plasticity--stability'' dilemma: Reinforcement Learning (RL) methods adapt well but lack stability guarantees, while control-theoretic approaches ensure stability but struggle with complex network dynamics. 
    In this paper, we propose a \textit{Lyapunov-Inspired Future-Aware PPO} agent that bridges this gap. Unlike standard RL that blindly optimizes rewards, our method incorporates a Lyapunov-inspired reward shaping mechanism. Instead of solving computationally heavy Constrained MDPs (CMDP), we inject a virtual queue drift penalty directly into the reward function to implicitly bias the policy toward buffer-safe regimes without adding inference-time overhead. Furthermore, we augment the state space with ``immediate future knowledge'' (next-chunk sizes and scene complexity), enabling the agent to anticipate stochastic throughput drops.
    Extensive experiments on real-world network traces demonstrate that our proposed agent drastically solves the plasticity-stability dilemma. It achieves an unprecedented level of stability, averaging only 1.5 quality switches per video compared to 17.8 in RobustMPC, while maintaining a highly competitive Mean QoE. Furthermore, zero-shot evaluations on highly volatile 5G mmWave networks prove our agent's real-world robustness. While baseline methods suffer catastrophic failures (up to 129 seconds of rebuffering) during sudden signal blockages, our Lyapunov-guided policy bounds the rebuffering risk to acceptable limits, successfully maintaining the highest practical QoE.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Adaptive Bitrate Streaming, Deep Reinforcement Learning, Lyapunov Optimization, QoE, Future-Awareness.
\end{IEEEkeywords}

% =========================================
% SECTION I: INTRODUCTION
% =========================================
\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

\IEEEPARstart{T}{he} exponential growth of video traffic, projected to account for over 82\% of global internet traffic \cite{cisco2023}, has fundamentally transformed the digital landscape. To cope with heterogeneous network conditions and diverse device capabilities, HTTP Adaptive Streaming (HAS) protocols, such as MPEG-DASH and HLS, have become the de-facto standard \cite{sodagar2011mpeg, bentaleb2018survey}. In HAS, a video is segmented into short chunks (e.g., 2-4 seconds), each encoded at multiple discrete bitrate levels. The core responsibility of the client-side player is to dynamically select the optimal bitrate for the next chunk, a decision process known as Adaptive Bitrate (ABR) selection.

The primary objective of an ABR algorithm is to maximize the user's Quality of Experience (QoE) \cite{wu2024qoe}. However, this poses a complex optimization problem involving conflicting goals: (1) maximizing the average visual quality (bitrate/VMAF), (2) minimizing rebuffering events (video stalls due to empty buffer), and (3) minimizing playback instability (frequent quality switching). Achieving an optimal balance remains particularly challenging in modern heterogeneous networks, such as \textbf{mmWave 5G \cite{meng2024mmwave} and Low Earth Orbit (LEO) satellite constellations (e.g., Starlink) \cite{zhang2024satellite}}, where throughput is highly stochastic and exhibits rapid, extreme fluctuations that traditional methods struggle to predict. Consequently, standard linear QoE metrics based strictly on mathematical averages are fundamentally flawed for modern evaluation; they fail to penalize worst-case visual degradation, necessitating a shift toward tail-risk metrics like Conditional Value at Risk (CVaR).

Historically, ABR algorithms have evolved through three distinct phases. First, \textit{Fixed-Rule methods} such as Buffer-Based (BBA) \cite{huang2014buffer} and BOLA \cite{spiteri2018bola} relied on heuristic thresholds. While simple, they often fail to utilize available bandwidth efficiently.
Second, \textit{Control-Theoretic methods} like Model Predictive Control (MPC) \cite{yin2015control} framed ABR as a formal optimization problem over a sliding horizon. While MPC provides explicit horizon-based optimization, it does not inherently enforce buffer safety under stochastic throughput, and its performance heavily depends on accurate throughput prediction \cite{sun2016cs2p}. In volatile networks (e.g., 5G or LEO), prediction errors lead to suboptimal decisions---either overly conservative (low quality) or overly aggressive (rebuffering).

Recently, \textit{Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL)} \cite{sutton2018reinforcement} has emerged as a promising paradigm. Systems like Pensieve \cite{mao2017neural} and Oboe \cite{akhtar2018oboe} employ neural networks to learn ABR policies directly from experience, capturing complex patterns without explicit modeling.
However, pure DRL approaches suffer from the \textit{``Plasticity--Stability Dilemma''}. While they are highly plastic (adaptable) to maximizing average reward, they lack inherent stability guarantees. A standard RL agent, driven solely by reward maximization, may learn aggressive behaviors that risk buffer depletion in ``tail'' scenarios, leading to catastrophic stalls. Furthermore, most existing agents are ``future-blind''; they rely solely on past observations and remain unaware of the varying sizes or complexity of upcoming video chunks.

In this paper, we address these limitations by proposing a \textbf{Hybrid Control-RL Framework}. We argue that robust ABR streaming requires bridging the gap between the adaptability of learning-based methods and the safety guarantees of control theory. Specifically, we utilize \textit{Lyapunov Optimization Theory} \cite{neely2010stochastic}, a powerful tool for stabilizing queueing systems, to guide the exploration of a PPO (Proximal Policy Optimization) \cite{schulman2017proximal} agent.
Our specific contributions are as follows:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Lyapunov-Inspired Safety Regularization:} We introduce a Lyapunov-inspired penalty derived from queue deficit considerations. Instead of treating rebuffering merely as a negative scalar in the QoE formulation, this mechanism implicitly penalizes unsafe queue deviations. As shown in Theorem 1, this regularization promotes stability through bounded deficit regularization, creating a ``safe zone'' for the RL agent without the computational burden of complex dual-variable CMDP solvers.
    
    \item \textbf{Future-Aware State Space:} We address the ``blindness'' of standard RL by augmenting the state space with immediate future knowledge. By feeding the exact sizes and complexity indices (SI/TI) of the next $M$ chunks into the neural network, we eliminate uncertainty regarding content variations, allowing the agent to distinguish between network-induced and content-induced buffer fluctuations.
    
    \item \textbf{Superior QoE with Unprecedented Stability:} We conduct extensive trace-driven evaluations using the standard broadband datasets. Instead of claiming blanket linear QoE merely by gaming the reward function—which heuristic methods like BBA achieve strictly by operating unsafely with high stall risks and chaotic switching—we demonstrate a fundamentally superior trade-off. Our method outperforms both predictive control methods (RobustMPC) and pure DRL baselines (Pensieve) in overall QoE. Critically, it lowers the bitrate switching frequency by over 91\% compared to RobustMPC (from 17.8 down to 1.5 switches per video), delivering an unprecedented level of viewing stability.

    \item \textbf{Zero-Shot Generalization in 5G Networks:} We demonstrate the real-world deployability of our agent by testing it on extreme 5G mmWave traces without any fine-tuning. While traditional predictive methods (e.g., RobustMPC) suffer catastrophic failures (up to 95 seconds of rebuffering) during sudden signal blockages, our Lyapunov-guided policy robustly navigates these extreme drops, outperforming all operational baselines in overall QoE.
\end{itemize}

